where we additionally require the\n\ngraphs to have large minimum degree. Both of these problems turn out be intimately related to two other\n\nproblems regarding maximally independent sets of a given size in K 3-free graphs and generalisations of these\n\nproblems to K r with r≥4. The ﬁrst problem is to maximise the number of maximally independent sets\n\ngiven the number of vertices and the second problem is to minimise the number of edges given the number\n\nof maximally independent sets. They are interesting in their own right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-04-24&page=53&range=7)\nciteturn2search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; In this paper, we present a proof of the consistency of the New Foundations set theory ($\\mathit{NF}$). $\\mathit{NF}$'s main idea is to permit very large sets (including the Universal Set) by restricting set formation to stratified formulas, thereby avoiding the classic set-theoretic paradoxes. ... @misc{2504.14392, author = {Xinqun Mei and Guofang Wang and Liangjun Weng}, title = {{C}onvex capillary hypersurfaces of prescribed curvature problem}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2504.14392}, note = {arXiv:2504.14392v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... Apr 22 2025 math.CO arXiv:2504.14389v2\nThe problem was known to be NP-hard in general, and polynomial-time algorithms were found for some restricted graph classes. In this note we prove that the defensive domination problem is $\\Sigma^P_2$-complete. We also introduce a natural variant of the defensive domination problem in which the defense is allowed to be a multiset of vertices. This variant is also $\\Sigma^P_2$-complete, but we show that it admits a polynomial-time algorithm in the class of interval graphs. A similar result was known for the original setting in the class of proper interval graphs.\n\n  * A new intersection condition in extremal set theory\n\nKartal Nagy\n\nApr 22 2025 math.CO arXiv:2504.14389v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2504.14389, author = {Kartal Nagy}, title = {{A} new intersection condition in extremal set theory}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2504.14389}, note = {arXiv:2504.14389v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe call a family $\\mathcal{F}$ $(3,2,\\ell)$-intersecting if $|A \\cap B|+|B \\cap C|+|C \\cap A| \\geq \\ell$ for all $A$, $B$, $C \\in \\mathcal{F}$. We try to look for the maximum size of such a family $\\mathcal{F}$ in case when $\\mathcal{F} \\subset {[n] \\choose k}$ or $\\mathcal{F} \\subset 2^{[n]}$. In the uniform case we show that if $\\mathcal{F}$ is $(3,2,2)$-intersecting, then $\\vert \\mathcal{F} \\vert \\leq {n+1 \\choose k-1}+{n \\choose k-2}$ and if $\\mathcal{F}$ is $(3,2,3)$-intersecting, then $|\\mathcal{F}| \\leq {n \\choose k-1} + 2 {n \\choose k-3} + 3 {n-1 \\choose k-3}$. For the lower bound we construct a $(3,2,\\ell)$-intersecting family and we show that this bound is sharp when $\\ell=2$ or $3$ and $n$ is sufficiently large compared to $k$. In the non-uniform case we give an upper bound for a $(3,2,n-x)$-intersecting family, when $n$ is sufficiently large compared to $x$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Calbet_A in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ACalbet_A+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; We give a new elementary proof that avoids the use of the triangle removal lemma and improves the upper bound to $O\\left(n^{3-1/s}(\\log n)^{-1+1/s}\\right)$. ... Dec 02 2024 math.CO arXiv:2411.19267v1 ... @misc{2411.19267, author = {Asier Calbet}, title = {{T}win-free ${K}_r$-saturated {G}raphs and {M}aximally {I}ndependent {S}ets in ${K}_3$-free {G}raphs}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2411.19267}, note = {arXiv:2411.19267v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... This is often cited in the literature as the main progress towards proving Tuza's Conjecture.\n\nPDF\n\nWe consider the following generalized Turán problem: For $2 \\le s \\le t$, what is the maximum number of triangles in a $K_{1,s,t}$-free graph on $n$ vertices? The previously best known lower and upper bounds are $\\Omega(n^2)$ and $o(n^{3-1/s})$, respectively. To the best of our knowledge, all known proofs of the upper bound use the triangle removal lemma. We give a new elementary proof that avoids the use of the triangle removal lemma and improves the upper bound to $O\\left(n^{3-1/s}(\\log n)^{-1+1/s}\\right)$.\n\n  * Twin-free $K_r$-saturated Graphs and Maximally Independent Sets in $K_3$-free Graphs\n\nAsier Calbet\n\nDec 02 2024 math.CO arXiv:2411.19267v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2411.19267, author = {Asier Calbet}, title = {{T}win-free ${K}_r$-saturated {G}raphs and {M}aximally {I}ndependent {S}ets in ${K}_3$-free {G}raphs}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2411.19267}, note = {arXiv:2411.19267v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nErdős Matching Conjecture for uniform hypergraphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/375879/erdos-matching-conjecture-for-uniform-hypergraphs)\nciteturn2search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; The paper describes its main theorem as a spectral confirmation in the sufficiently large-$n$n regime, while the full extremal conjecture is not stated as completely resolved here. ... The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:2606.24529, arXiv:2604.19183, arXiv:2603.06415, arXiv:2511.21628, arXiv:2511.17000, arXiv:2508.17683, arXiv:2504.14389, arXiv:2404.09720, arXiv:2403.04289, arXiv:2012.15142, arXiv:2011.14252, arXiv:2002.06601, and 1 more.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nLiying Kang, Yongchun Lu, Xiying Yuan and Junpeng Zhou, “A Spectral Confirmation of the Erdős Matching Conjecture”, arXiv:2607.07392 (2026).\n\n## Additional references\n\n14 papers in this index state this conjecture (2018–2026). The state